<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="c" from="3" to="6" step="1"/>

  <function name="f">1/($c + x)</function>
  <derivative name="fP">$f</derivative>
  <derivative name="fPP">$fP</derivative>
  <derivative name="fPPP">$fPP</derivative>
  <derivative name="fP4">$fPPP</derivative>

  <function name="g">1/($c + x)^2</function>
  <derivative name="gP">$g</derivative>
  <derivative name="gPP">$gP</derivative>
  <derivative name="gPPP">$gPP</derivative>
  <derivative name="gP4">$gPPP</derivative>

  <math name="a">0</math>

  <function name="genTermf" variables="x n">1/$c*(-x/$c)^n</function>
  <function name="genTermg1" variables="x n">-n/$c^2*(-x/$c)^(n-1)</function>

</setup>

<p>
  Consider the function
  <me>
    f(x) = \frac{1}{$c + x}
  </me>
  In what follows, we will use the familiar Taylor series expansion for <m>\frac{1}{1-x}</m> to find the Taylor series for both <m>f(x)= \frac{1}{$c + x}</m> and <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{($c + x)^2}</m>.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Observe that
        <me>
          f(x) = \frac{1}{$c + x} = \frac{\frac{1}{$c}}{\frac{1}{$c}($c + x)} = \frac{\frac{1}{$c}}{1+\frac{x}{$c}} = \frac{1}{$c} \cdot \frac{1}{1+\frac{x}{$c}}
        </me>
        and this representation of <m>f(x)</m> has similar algebraic structure to <m>\frac{1}{1-x}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the familiar Taylor series expansion for <m>\frac{1}{1-x}</m> and an appropriate substitution to find the Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m> and to write the series in expanded and in sigma notation.
      </p>
      <p>
        State the first 4 nonzero terms of the Taylor series of <m>f</m> centered at <m>a = $a</m> by completing the blanks below; note that there is one blank per term and that several of the terms involve the variable <m>x</m>.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
        <m>f(x) = </m><answer><shortDescription>first term</shortDescription>$$f($a)</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>second term</shortDescription>$$fP($a)(x-$a)</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>third term</shortDescription>$$fPP($a)/2! * (x-$a)^2</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>fourth term</shortDescription>$$fPPP($a)/3! * (x-$a)^3</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        State the Taylor series of <m>f</m> centered at <m>a = $a</m> in sigma notation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} </m><answer><shortDescription>general term of f</shortDescription>$genTermf</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Based on the fact that the Taylor series for <m>\frac{1}{1-x}</m> converges on the open interval <m>(-1,1)</m>, determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{$c + x}</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter (-infinity, infinity).
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>f(x)</m>  <answer><label>converges on</label><interval>(-$c,$c)</interval></answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Now consider the function
        <me>
          g(x) = \frac{1}{($c+x)^2}
        </me>
        which seems closely related to the function <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{$c+x}</m>.  First, determine <m>f'(x)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>f'(x) = </m></label>$fP</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Notice that <m>g(x)</m> is a constant multiple of <m>f'(x)</m>.  In particular, 
      </p>
      <p>
       <m>g(x) =</m> <mathInput name="theConstant" minWidth="35"><shortDescription>the needed constant</shortDescription></mathInput><m>f'(x)</m><answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$theConstant=-1</when></award></answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        By observing the relationship between <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> and using the Taylor series you found in part (a), find the Taylor series for <m>g(x)</m> and  write the series in expanded and in sigma notation.
      </p>
      <p>
        State the first 4 nonzero terms of the Taylor series of <m>g</m> centered at <m>a = $a</m> by completing the blanks below; note that there is one blank per term and that several of the terms involve the variable <m>x</m>.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
        <m>g(x) = </m><answer><shortDescription>first term</shortDescription>$$g($a)</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>second term</shortDescription>$$gP($a)(x-$a)</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>third term</shortDescription>$$gPP($a)/2! * (x-$a)^2</answer><m>+</m><answer><shortDescription>fourth term</shortDescription>$$gPPP($a)/3! * (x-$a)^3</answer><m>+ \cdots</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        State the Taylor series of <m>g</m> centered at <m>a = $a</m> in sigma notation.  Note that the index for the sum starts at <m>n=1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} </m><answer><shortDescription>general term g</shortDescription>$genTermg1</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Apply the Power Series Differentiation and Integration Theorem to determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{($c + x)^2}</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter (-infinity, infinity).
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>g(x)</m>  <answer><label>converges on</label><interval>(-$c,$c)</interval></answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>